WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-IN

00:00:00.001 --> 00:00:00.992
In this video,

00:00:01.017 --> 00:00:04.516
we will do a complete discussion on
the top three health insurance policies.

00:00:04.682 --> 00:00:11.478
We will also compare some of the best health
insurance policies available as of today.

00:00:11.502 --> 00:00:16.160
For example, we will compare
HDFC Ergo with Care Supreme.

00:00:16.350 --> 00:00:18.969
Then we’ll compare these with Niva Bupa.

00:00:18.993 --> 00:00:23.968
Similarly, we’ll compare them with Aditya
Birla, Bajaj Allianz, and ICICI Lombard.

00:00:24.214 --> 00:00:27.610
In short, these are some of the best
health insurance policies available today,

00:00:27.879 --> 00:00:30.522
and we will compare all
of them with each other

00:00:30.546 --> 00:00:32.419
and finally arrive at a conclusion

00:00:32.443 --> 00:00:35.831
about which three are the top
health insurance policies at present.

00:00:36.291 --> 00:00:37.656
But do you know what the challenge is?

00:00:37.680 --> 00:00:41.933
If I start comparing these policies
right at the beginning of the video,

00:00:42.107 --> 00:00:44.107
about half of the people
will understand everything,

00:00:44.344 --> 00:00:47.083
but for the other half, it will
be very difficult to understand.

00:00:47.250 --> 00:00:53.940
The reason is that many people are
not familiar with health insurance terms—

00:00:53.965 --> 00:00:58.603
like what restoration benefit means, what
pre- and post-hospitalization expenses are,

00:00:58.872 --> 00:01:00.079
what co-pay is, and so on.

00:01:00.103 --> 00:01:03.618
Until you understand these
health insurance terms,

00:01:03.808 --> 00:01:06.371
there’s no benefit in comparing policies.

00:01:06.673 --> 00:01:08.347
So I figured out the best way to do this.

00:01:08.371 --> 00:01:10.791
I picked one random
health insurance policy.

00:01:11.259 --> 00:01:14.211
In this video, I will teach you how
to read a health insurance policy.

00:01:14.576 --> 00:01:17.353
Once you learn how to read
a health insurance policy,

00:01:17.377 --> 00:01:22.130
you’ll be able to review any
policy, identify its key features,

00:01:22.399 --> 00:01:25.566
and decide whether
it’s right for you or not.

00:01:25.773 --> 00:01:29.693
I’ve picked a random policy, and I
won’t even mention its name here.

00:01:29.717 --> 00:01:34.367
If I write the policy name, people
start commenting that it’s a paid video.

00:01:34.556 --> 00:01:37.104
So I haven’t written the name—I've
just taken screenshots of its features.

00:01:37.128 --> 00:01:41.255
Look at the screen once; I’ve added
screenshots of all these features here.

00:01:41.644 --> 00:01:43.739
I’ll teach you how to read this policy.

00:01:43.763 --> 00:01:49.024
For example, suppose this is one
of its benefits—restoration of cover,

00:01:49.048 --> 00:01:51.571
where it is offering a
restoration benefit of ₹25 lakh.

00:01:51.595 --> 00:01:55.055
Along with that, I’ll explain the feature
and also explain what it actually means.

00:01:55.079 --> 00:01:57.079
What will happen is that your
knowledge will keep increasing,

00:01:57.285 --> 00:01:59.579
the health insurance topic will
become completely clear to you,

00:02:00.039 --> 00:02:02.269
and then whenever you pick
up any health insurance policy,

00:02:02.293 --> 00:02:04.824
you’ll understand everything easily.

00:02:04.990 --> 00:02:06.474
We’ll start from basics.

00:02:06.498 --> 00:02:11.405
Just one thing I want to mention: the
policy I’ve chosen here is a women’s policy.

00:02:11.429 --> 00:02:14.381
This policy can be taken only
by women; men cannot buy it.

00:02:14.406 --> 00:02:16.388
I’ve chosen this policy
specifically because

00:02:16.413 --> 00:02:20.102
women’s policies come with an
additional benefit—maternity benefit—

00:02:20.126 --> 00:02:21.808
which is not available in men’s policies.

00:02:21.832 --> 00:02:23.442
I wanted to explain that benefit as well,

00:02:23.466 --> 00:02:25.648
because for women, this
benefit is very important.

00:02:25.743 --> 00:02:27.743
That’s the only reason
I selected this policy.

00:02:27.767 --> 00:02:31.203
I’m assuming that everyone is a
beginner—that nobody knows anything yet.

00:02:31.227 --> 00:02:33.227
That’s the approach I’m using to teach you.

00:02:33.489 --> 00:02:35.663
Now look at this coverage section.

00:02:35.687 --> 00:02:37.083
What’s the first thing written here?

00:02:37.107 --> 00:02:38.447
Coverage: ₹25 lakh.

00:02:38.471 --> 00:02:43.240
When I took this screenshot, I had selected
a health insurance coverage of ₹25 lakh.

00:02:43.390 --> 00:02:46.811
You can choose ₹5 lakh, ₹10 lakh,
₹20 lakh, or even ₹1 crore—

00:02:46.835 --> 00:02:47.930
that choice is completely yours.

00:02:48.010 --> 00:02:49.771
I’m using ₹25 lakh just as an example.

00:02:49.796 --> 00:02:52.247
The premium was ₹12,000.

00:02:52.271 --> 00:02:53.850
I have missed adding that screenshot here.

00:02:54.325 --> 00:02:55.341
What this means is that

00:02:55.365 --> 00:03:00.460
I have to pay ₹12,000 to my health
insurance company once every year.

00:03:00.484 --> 00:03:03.405
Once a year, I pay ₹12,000 to the company.

00:03:03.627 --> 00:03:04.524
The good thing is that

00:03:04.548 --> 00:03:08.421
the government has now reduced GST
on health insurance and term plans to 0%.

00:03:08.722 --> 00:03:13.849
Earlier, if this policy cost ₹12,000, an
additional 18% GST was also charged on it.

00:03:14.008 --> 00:03:15.397
Now GST has become zero.

00:03:15.421 --> 00:03:18.103
So this is a very good advantage

00:03:18.213 --> 00:03:20.476
the government has given on
health insurance and term plans.

00:03:21.468 --> 00:03:22.960
So the premium here is ₹12,000.

00:03:22.991 --> 00:03:27.412
That means I pay ₹12,000 per year to my
health insurance company, and in return,

00:03:27.436 --> 00:03:32.777
the company provides me with a
health insurance cover of ₹25 lakh.

00:03:32.919 --> 00:03:37.459
₹25 lakh cover means that if tomorrow
I get hospitalized due to any illness,

00:03:37.594 --> 00:03:39.911
I am medically covered up to ₹25 lakh.

00:03:39.935 --> 00:03:44.117
My hospital bills and related expenses will
be paid by the health insurance company.

00:03:44.576 --> 00:03:47.664
Now let’s talk about the
first benefit—room rent limit.

00:03:47.807 --> 00:03:49.251
Under room rent limit, this policy says

00:03:49.275 --> 00:03:52.362
you can choose any
category of room except suites.

00:03:52.465 --> 00:03:55.782
Suites are typically for ultra-rich
people, so we can ignore that option.

00:03:55.807 --> 00:03:58.124
Otherwise, you can
choose any room category.

00:03:58.148 --> 00:04:02.433
Whether you take a single
sharing room, double sharing room,

00:04:02.457 --> 00:04:04.909
or even get admitted to a
general ward—it doesn’t matter.

00:04:04.933 --> 00:04:07.163
Whatever the bill amount
is, the company will pay it.

00:04:07.536 --> 00:04:09.758
The second benefit is restoration of cover,

00:04:09.790 --> 00:04:11.972
under which it is offering a
restoration benefit of ₹25 lakh.

00:04:12.250 --> 00:04:15.416
This ₹25 lakh restoration is because
the policy coverage itself is ₹25 lakh.

00:04:15.440 --> 00:04:16.987
If my policy coverage were ₹1 crore,

00:04:17.011 --> 00:04:19.011
then the restoration of
cover would also be ₹1 crore.

00:04:19.931 --> 00:04:22.288
What does restoration
of cover actually mean?

00:04:22.312 --> 00:04:26.288
Let’s say there is a woman
who has a ₹25 lakh policy.

00:04:26.541 --> 00:04:32.496
God forbid, she develops a kidney-related
problem and gets admitted to the hospital,

00:04:32.520 --> 00:04:35.250
and suppose the expense comes to ₹20 lakh.

00:04:35.583 --> 00:04:38.591
Then, imagine that after one
or two months in the same year,

00:04:38.615 --> 00:04:43.321
she develops a lung-related issue and
the expense comes to another ₹10 lakh.

00:04:43.638 --> 00:04:46.107
Technically, her total medical
expense in that year becomes ₹30 lakh,

00:04:46.131 --> 00:04:48.131
while her policy coverage is only ₹25 lakh.

00:04:48.282 --> 00:04:50.282
Normally, the insurance
company would pay ₹25 lakh,

00:04:50.306 --> 00:04:53.036
and she would have to pay the remaining
₹5 lakh from her own pocket, right?

00:04:53.274 --> 00:04:57.321
But this policy also provides
a restoration of cover benefit.

00:04:57.345 --> 00:05:01.210
That means she gets an additional restoration
cover of ₹25 lakh under this policy.

00:05:02.019 --> 00:05:06.487
So, when she fell ill for the first
time, she had a ₹25 lakh policy, right?

00:05:06.827 --> 00:05:08.081
How much was the expense?

00:05:08.106 --> 00:05:08.970
₹20 lakh.

00:05:08.994 --> 00:05:14.367
As soon as this ₹20 lakh expense
is claimed, her policy gets restored.

00:05:14.581 --> 00:05:16.494
How many times does this
restoration happen in a year?

00:05:16.518 --> 00:05:19.121
Once in a year—only one time per year.

00:05:19.256 --> 00:05:25.219
So once the ₹20 lakh expense
is settled, she comes back home,

00:05:25.243 --> 00:05:28.710
and her policy gets restored
once again to ₹25 lakh.

00:05:28.734 --> 00:05:31.400
Now suppose that two months
later, she develops a lung-related issue

00:05:31.424 --> 00:05:33.280
and the expense comes to ₹10 lakh.

00:05:33.304 --> 00:05:34.724
In that case, she doesn’t have to
pay anything from her own pocket—

00:05:34.748 --> 00:05:35.946
the insurance company will pay it.

00:05:35.970 --> 00:05:39.493
After this claim, the remaining
policy balance will be ₹15 lakh.

00:05:39.785 --> 00:05:41.158
One more important thing is written here:

00:05:41.182 --> 00:05:45.539
the restoration benefit is applicable
for both related and unrelated illnesses.

00:05:45.563 --> 00:05:48.118
That means the policy was ₹25 lakh.

00:05:48.142 --> 00:05:50.142
₹20 lakh was spent on
a kidney-related issue.

00:05:50.332 --> 00:05:54.245
If another ₹10 lakh had been
spent again on the kidney,

00:05:54.277 --> 00:05:56.451
it would mean the same related disease.

00:05:57.117 --> 00:05:59.617
But this time, how did the expense happen?

00:05:59.641 --> 00:06:00.831
First, there was a kidney-related issue,

00:06:00.855 --> 00:06:03.378
and then there was an unrelated
illness—a lung-related problem.

00:06:03.402 --> 00:06:07.592
So it clearly states that whether
the illness is related or unrelated,

00:06:07.767 --> 00:06:10.274
the restoration benefit
will be provided in full.

00:06:10.487 --> 00:06:11.885
Some policies work differently.

00:06:11.909 --> 00:06:13.091
For example, suppose the cover is ₹25 lakh

00:06:13.115 --> 00:06:15.845
and ₹20 lakh is spent
on a kidney-related issue.

00:06:15.996 --> 00:06:18.932
Those policies say that if the
kidney problem occurs again,

00:06:18.956 --> 00:06:20.606
restoration benefit will not be given.

00:06:20.788 --> 00:06:24.590
Restoration benefit will be provided only
if the next illness is an unrelated one—

00:06:24.614 --> 00:06:27.717
like a lung issue or a heart problem.

00:06:27.971 --> 00:06:28.804
But this policy clearly states that

00:06:28.828 --> 00:06:30.454
the restoration benefit applies whether
the illness is related or unrelated.

00:06:30.478 --> 00:06:34.095
And this type of restoration
benefit is always better.

00:06:34.120 --> 00:06:35.646
Because some policies say that

00:06:35.670 --> 00:06:37.595
they will provide the benefit
only if the illness is unrelated.

00:06:37.619 --> 00:06:39.920
The reason is simple: if
someone has a kidney problem,

00:06:39.944 --> 00:06:42.968
the probability is much higher that the
issue will occur again in the kidney itself.

00:06:42.992 --> 00:06:46.222
It’s not very likely that a person
undergoing dialysis for a kidney problem

00:06:46.317 --> 00:06:48.579
will suddenly develop a lung issue.

00:06:48.603 --> 00:06:50.603
Most likely, the kidney
problem will occur again.

00:06:50.627 --> 00:06:55.008
That’s why a restoration benefit that
covers both related and unrelated illnesses

00:06:55.032 --> 00:06:56.492
is much better.

00:06:58.206 --> 00:07:02.024
Next comes the renewal bonus, which
is also called NCB—No Claim Bonus.

00:07:02.048 --> 00:07:05.372
This policy says you will get
a bonus of ₹5 lakh per year,

00:07:05.530 --> 00:07:08.490
with a maximum benefit of ₹25 lakh.

00:07:08.515 --> 00:07:11.474
Let’s say you have taken a
₹25 lakh health insurance policy.

00:07:12.617 --> 00:07:14.998
Throughout the entire year,
you stay completely fit and fine,

00:07:15.022 --> 00:07:17.680
you are not hospitalized even
once, and you don’t make any claim.

00:07:17.774 --> 00:07:19.386
Now it’s time to renew the policy.

00:07:19.410 --> 00:07:21.839
You again pay the premium
of ₹12,000 for the next year.

00:07:22.323 --> 00:07:25.413
Now you will receive a
no-claim bonus of ₹5 lakh.

00:07:25.921 --> 00:07:31.043
Since you did not make any claim,
the premium remains the same.

00:07:31.067 --> 00:07:35.018
As a result, your policy coverage
will increase from ₹25 lakh to ₹30 lakh.

00:07:35.265 --> 00:07:36.907
If you again don’t make
any claim the following year,

00:07:36.931 --> 00:07:39.605
you’ll get another ₹5 lakh bonus,
and your coverage becomes ₹35 lakh.

00:07:39.629 --> 00:07:41.660
This continues until the maximum
bonus limit is reached, which is ₹25 lakh.

00:07:41.684 --> 00:07:44.358
It’s not like if you don’t make a claim for
five years, the bonus becomes ₹25 lakh,

00:07:44.382 --> 00:07:47.095
and then if you don’t make a claim in the
sixth year, it will increase to ₹30 lakh.

00:07:47.253 --> 00:07:49.119
It doesn’t work like 30 plus 25.

00:07:49.167 --> 00:07:51.023
The maximum bonus is ₹25 lakh,

00:07:51.047 --> 00:07:53.395
which means your total policy
coverage can go up to ₹50 lakh at most.

00:07:54.323 --> 00:07:56.323
Now suppose you start taking claims again.

00:07:56.347 --> 00:07:59.100
Let’s say you didn’t take
any claim for five years,

00:07:59.124 --> 00:08:00.282
and in the sixth year you take a claim.

00:08:00.368 --> 00:08:03.004
It doesn’t mean that your entire
no-claim bonus will become zero.

00:08:03.028 --> 00:08:04.464
It also doesn’t mean that
as soon as you take a claim,

00:08:04.488 --> 00:08:05.893
your policy will come back to ₹25 lakh.

00:08:06.305 --> 00:08:08.702
As soon as you make a claim,
only ₹5 lakh will be reduced,

00:08:08.726 --> 00:08:10.679
and the policy coverage will come
down to ₹45 lakh in the next year.

00:08:10.703 --> 00:08:12.560
So if your coverage had
increased year by year,

00:08:12.584 --> 00:08:14.088
it will reduce in the
same way— year by year—

00:08:14.113 --> 00:08:15.536
whenever you take a claim.

00:08:16.176 --> 00:08:17.070
Next comes co-pay.

00:08:17.211 --> 00:08:20.148
Co-pay simply means sharing, and
ideally, you should never opt for it.

00:08:20.498 --> 00:08:23.578
In this policy, everything is 100% paid
by the insurer, which is a good thing.

00:08:23.776 --> 00:08:24.761
What does co-pay actually mean?

00:08:24.786 --> 00:08:26.832
Some policies say something like this:

00:08:27.022 --> 00:08:29.022
suppose you have a ₹25 lakh
health insurance policy,

00:08:29.625 --> 00:08:31.625
and the premium is ₹12,000 per year.

00:08:32.736 --> 00:08:35.292
Now the same company
says, ‘Don’t pay ₹12,000.

00:08:35.316 --> 00:08:37.149
Just pay ₹8,000 as the annual premium.’

00:08:37.235 --> 00:08:39.909
Naturally, this sounds very attractive,

00:08:40.639 --> 00:08:42.774
and you might think it’s a
great deal and agree to it.

00:08:42.798 --> 00:08:44.798
But then the company adds a condition.

00:08:44.822 --> 00:08:48.139
It says that there will be a 10%
co-pay, which means ₹2.5 lakh.

00:08:48.488 --> 00:08:55.243
So if you get hospitalized and your
medical bill comes up to ₹2.5 lakh,

00:08:55.267 --> 00:08:56.846
you will have to pay the
entire amount yourself.

00:08:56.997 --> 00:08:58.997
Only if the bill exceeds ₹2.5 lakh will
the insurance company start paying.

00:08:59.211 --> 00:09:00.290
So what’s the catch here?

00:09:00.314 --> 00:09:02.853
The lower premium creates temptation.

00:09:03.519 --> 00:09:08.345
Companies usually say, ‘Your
pocket can easily handle ₹2.5 lakh.

00:09:08.369 --> 00:09:11.964
You’re taking health insurance
only for very big medical bills anyway.’

00:09:12.266 --> 00:09:14.353
And this is how many people get trapped.

00:09:14.511 --> 00:09:16.670
That’s why I would suggest choosing
a policy where the co-pay is such that

00:09:16.694 --> 00:09:18.328
100% of the expense is paid
by the insurance company.

00:09:18.352 --> 00:09:21.820
We buy health insurance for peace of mind.

00:09:22.185 --> 00:09:23.598
There should be no calculations involved.

00:09:24.487 --> 00:09:25.947
The rule is simple:

00:09:25.971 --> 00:09:35.428
choose a co-pay option where the entire
amount is paid by the insurer—no ifs and buts.

00:09:36.365 --> 00:09:38.119
After that comes the OPD benefit.

00:09:38.237 --> 00:09:44.525
This means that if you visit a doctor and
get a prescription—say for ₹400 or ₹500—

00:09:44.549 --> 00:09:45.628
that expense is also covered.

00:09:45.652 --> 00:09:49.469
Generally, in most health insurance
policies—especially a ₹25 lakh policy—

00:09:49.684 --> 00:09:58.115
the claim is paid only when you are admitted
to the hospital for more than 24 hours.

00:09:58.139 --> 00:09:59.686
But nowadays, many additional conditions
and features have been introduced.

00:09:59.710 --> 00:10:01.320
I’ll explain all of those
features to you as well.

00:10:02.494 --> 00:10:03.986
How much is the OPD benefit?

00:10:04.010 --> 00:10:06.010
This policy is offering
an OPD benefit of ₹3,500.

00:10:06.035 --> 00:10:07.675
Read along with me.

00:10:07.700 --> 00:10:11.319
Up to ₹3,500 is available, and it is
only for the female insured person.

00:10:11.343 --> 00:10:13.263
As I mentioned earlier,
this policy is only for women,

00:10:13.287 --> 00:10:13.929
which is why this benefit applies.

00:10:13.953 --> 00:10:17.064
So, you will get a maximum
OPD benefit of ₹3,500 in a year.

00:10:17.381 --> 00:10:19.595
You cannot say that since
your overall cover is ₹25 lakh,

00:10:19.619 --> 00:10:20.833
the OPD expenses will
also come from that amount.

00:10:20.857 --> 00:10:22.008
There is a separate limit—

00:10:22.032 --> 00:10:24.683
only ₹3,500 for OPD expenses like doctor
consultation slips and prescriptions.

00:10:24.802 --> 00:10:26.873
If you get admitted to the
hospital and undergo treatment,

00:10:26.897 --> 00:10:28.746
then the full ₹25 lakh coverage applies.

00:10:28.968 --> 00:10:33.007
Now this is a very important
part—waiting periods.

00:10:33.134 --> 00:10:35.943
This is called PED, which
stands for pre-existing disease.

00:10:35.967 --> 00:10:38.816
Suppose, God forbid,
someone is already ill.

00:10:38.840 --> 00:10:42.633
For example, if a person already has a
kidney-related problem and is on medication,

00:10:42.743 --> 00:10:47.664
and now wants to purchase a health
insurance policy—let’s say this policy.

00:10:48.124 --> 00:10:53.401
When they buy this policy, a waiting period
for pre-existing diseases will apply.

00:10:53.425 --> 00:10:54.115
How long is it?

00:10:54.139 --> 00:10:57.448
The waiting period for
existing illnesses is two years.

00:10:57.472 --> 00:11:03.029
That means, just as an example,
if today’s date is 1 January 2026

00:11:03.053 --> 00:11:06.203
and the person buys the policy
today, then for the next two years,

00:11:06.314 --> 00:11:10.290
any kidney-related
issue will not be covered.

00:11:10.505 --> 00:11:14.830
The two-year period will be
completed on 1 January 2028.

00:11:14.854 --> 00:11:20.361
After that, even if a kidney-related problem
occurs, this policy will provide coverage.

00:11:20.829 --> 00:11:24.583
Some policies have a
waiting period of just one year.

00:11:24.607 --> 00:11:26.273
This one has a two-year waiting period.

00:11:26.519 --> 00:11:28.813
Some policies have a
waiting period of just one year.

00:11:28.955 --> 00:11:32.105
And some policies even have
a waiting period of four years.

00:11:33.295 --> 00:11:39.418
Now one important thing: you cannot
simply look at any policy and say that

00:11:39.442 --> 00:11:42.053
this is the best health
insurance policy today,

00:11:42.124 --> 00:11:45.259
or that these are the top
three health insurance policies.

00:11:45.988 --> 00:11:49.552
A policy that is best for one
person may not be best for another.

00:11:49.845 --> 00:11:55.520
For example, if someone already has a
medical condition—say a kidney problem—

00:11:55.774 --> 00:11:59.964
then a policy with a one-year waiting
period would be best for them, right?

00:12:00.242 --> 00:12:05.973
But if someone is completely
fit and fine, they might say,

00:12:05.997 --> 00:12:10.330
‘This policy has a four-year waiting period,
but its other features are very good.’

00:12:10.354 --> 00:12:11.298
In that case, they may not
mind the longer waiting period

00:12:11.322 --> 00:12:12.290
because they don’t expect
to make a claim immediately.

00:12:12.314 --> 00:12:13.837
For them, that policy
could be the best choice.

00:12:14.885 --> 00:12:19.099
So which health insurance
policy is best for you

00:12:19.123 --> 00:12:23.908
completely depends on your exact needs.

00:12:24.852 --> 00:12:27.923
Many people get confused
about how to figure this out.

00:12:27.947 --> 00:12:31.685
In my opinion, the best way to
do this is through Ditto Insurance.

00:12:32.003 --> 00:12:34.748
The reason is—let me show
you their interface once.

00:12:34.772 --> 00:12:38.636
With Ditto Insurance, you can
get completely spam-free advice.

00:12:38.660 --> 00:12:45.430
This means you can talk to the Ditto team
anytime and ask them any questions you have.

00:12:45.454 --> 00:12:49.485
If you have any illness, you
can tell their team about it,

00:12:49.509 --> 00:12:52.556
and they will suggest
which policy is best for you.

00:12:52.936 --> 00:12:59.351
I’m stressing on ‘spam-free’ because with
many vendors and insurance companies,

00:12:59.375 --> 00:13:02.938
the moment you enter your phone number,
they start bothering you with calls.

00:13:03.239 --> 00:13:04.477
Here, it’s spam-free.

00:13:04.501 --> 00:13:07.953
You explain your needs once, and even
if you don’t fully understand the policy,

00:13:07.977 --> 00:13:10.318
you won’t receive repeated
calls from different companies.

00:13:10.985 --> 00:13:15.815
Now let me show you how
to take advice from them.

00:13:15.840 --> 00:13:17.356
I’ll put the link in the description.

00:13:17.697 --> 00:13:21.189
As soon as you click on that link,
you’ll land on Ditto’s official page.

00:13:21.452 --> 00:13:23.832
From there, you just need
to click on ‘Schedule a Call.’

00:13:23.856 --> 00:13:27.887
After clicking on ‘Schedule a
Call,’ you select a suitable time.

00:13:28.117 --> 00:13:31.108
If you have any questions
related to health insurance,

00:13:31.274 --> 00:13:34.560
you go to ‘Schedule a
date for health insurance.’

00:13:34.584 --> 00:13:38.401
For example, today is
the 4th, and I select the 5th.

00:13:38.425 --> 00:13:42.369
Then I book any available
slot—say 12:30—and confirm it.

00:13:42.456 --> 00:13:45.099
After that, you enter your
name, email ID, phone number,

00:13:45.123 --> 00:13:48.638
and ‘How did you hear about
Ditto’—you can fill in anything there.

00:13:48.717 --> 00:13:50.987
Then, in ‘Briefly describe your query,’

00:13:51.011 --> 00:13:53.320
you enter whatever question
you have and confirm it.

00:13:53.605 --> 00:13:58.010
The Ditto team will call you, and
you can ask them all your doubts.

00:13:58.034 --> 00:14:02.851
Also, this consultation with the
Ditto team is completely free of cost.

00:14:02.875 --> 00:14:07.764
It’s not like you have to pay anything
just to talk to them—it’s totally free.

00:14:07.962 --> 00:14:11.271
And in case you ever need to
make a health insurance claim

00:14:11.295 --> 00:14:16.883
or face any issues or challenges, the
Ditto team is always ready to support you.

00:14:16.907 --> 00:14:20.589
The process is the same—you
can talk to the Ditto team anytime.

00:14:20.613 --> 00:14:23.636
They also provide complete
support during the claim process.

00:14:23.660 --> 00:14:25.826
Anyway, let’s come back to our topic.

00:14:25.850 --> 00:14:29.453
So far, we’ve understood
that for pre-existing diseases,

00:14:29.477 --> 00:14:33.088
some companies have a waiting
period of two years, some have one year,

00:14:33.112 --> 00:14:34.310
and some even have four years.

00:14:34.572 --> 00:14:38.286
Now look at what’s written below—an
initial waiting period of 30 days.

00:14:38.405 --> 00:14:39.507
What does this mean?

00:14:39.728 --> 00:14:43.586
Every health insurance policy has
an initial waiting period of 30 days.

00:14:43.610 --> 00:14:48.570
For example, suppose
today’s date is 1 January 2026.

00:14:49.276 --> 00:14:51.086
A person buys a health
insurance policy today

00:14:51.110 --> 00:14:55.364
and is completely fit and
fine, with no existing illness.

00:14:55.769 --> 00:15:00.673
But if within the first 30
days after buying the policy,

00:15:00.697 --> 00:15:02.697
they develop a kidney-related problem,

00:15:03.125 --> 00:15:07.244
then this health insurance policy
will not pay even ₹1 as a claim.

00:15:07.506 --> 00:15:08.339
What’s the reason for this?

00:15:08.584 --> 00:15:12.585
Health insurance companies are
worried that when people buy a policy,

00:15:12.815 --> 00:15:15.228
many of them don’t tell the truth.

00:15:15.252 --> 00:15:17.252
They say they have no medical
issues, even when they do.

00:15:17.474 --> 00:15:19.352
For example, someone may
already have a kidney problem

00:15:19.377 --> 00:15:20.717
and might be planning a surgery.

00:15:20.852 --> 00:15:23.042
They buy a health insurance
policy and within 5–7 days,

00:15:23.066 --> 00:15:24.962
they go ahead with the
surgery and raise a claim.

00:15:24.986 --> 00:15:27.485
That’s why insurance companies
say that even if you are fit and fine,

00:15:27.659 --> 00:15:31.056
they will not give any
claim within the first 30 days.

00:15:31.373 --> 00:15:34.627
It’s clearly written here: there
is a 30-day initial waiting period,

00:15:34.651 --> 00:15:37.444
except for claims
arising due to an accident.

00:15:37.468 --> 00:15:41.595
So if someone buys a policy and
meets with an accident the next day,

00:15:41.865 --> 00:15:43.508
there is no problem—the claim will be paid.

00:15:43.532 --> 00:15:47.221
Because it’s not possible that someone is
already bleeding or injured from an accident

00:15:47.245 --> 00:15:49.496
and then goes to buy a
health insurance policy.

00:15:49.593 --> 00:15:55.285
That’s why accident-related claims
within the first 30 days are covered.

00:15:55.554 --> 00:15:59.808
One important thing we discussed earlier
is that some people take policies by lying.

00:16:00.141 --> 00:16:01.339
Never do that.

00:16:01.466 --> 00:16:03.942
When I bought my mother’s
health insurance policy,

00:16:03.966 --> 00:16:05.966
my mother had a mild blood pressure issue.

00:16:06.053 --> 00:16:10.403
It wasn’t very serious—sometimes
she took BP medicine,

00:16:10.427 --> 00:16:12.283
sometimes she didn’t
for three or four months.

00:16:12.307 --> 00:16:15.052
If she ate fried food like samosas,

00:16:15.233 --> 00:16:17.233
she would take the medicine;
otherwise, she wouldn’t.

00:16:17.495 --> 00:16:20.400
This was around four years ago.

00:16:20.424 --> 00:16:23.987
When we were buying the policy,
I mentioned the BP condition.

00:16:24.241 --> 00:16:28.455
My mother asked me why I mentioned it,
saying she didn’t really have BP problems.

00:16:28.724 --> 00:16:30.621
But the truth is—never lie.

00:16:30.804 --> 00:16:34.860
There is no such thing as “occasional”
when it comes to medical disclosure.

00:16:35.185 --> 00:16:39.966
When a policy asks
whether you consume alcohol,

00:16:40.537 --> 00:16:45.291
some people select ‘No’ and think, ‘I drink
once every six months, so I’ll choose No.’

00:16:45.426 --> 00:16:47.786
Either you drink or you don’t.

00:16:48.279 --> 00:16:51.024
The same applies to smoking—some
people say they smoke occasionally.

00:16:51.706 --> 00:16:53.349
Either you smoke or you don’t.

00:16:53.374 --> 00:16:54.635
There is no “occasional” option here.

00:16:54.735 --> 00:16:56.497
You won’t select “No” for occasional use.

00:16:56.824 --> 00:16:58.298
If you have BP, then mention it.

00:16:58.322 --> 00:17:01.250
Taking medicine once every three
or four months doesn’t change that.

00:17:01.845 --> 00:17:04.924
The reason is simple: if today
you lie and say you don’t have BP,

00:17:05.035 --> 00:17:08.027
and tomorrow—God forbid—you
develop a heart-related problem,

00:17:08.051 --> 00:17:10.789
the doctor may mention that you had BP.

00:17:10.907 --> 00:17:15.138
If you didn’t disclose it while buying the
policy, your entire claim can be rejected.

00:17:15.162 --> 00:17:16.456
Then what’s the point of paying premiums?

00:17:16.480 --> 00:17:18.480
Ultimately, when you
actually need the policy,

00:17:18.607 --> 00:17:22.432
the insurance company will find
some ‘if and but’ to deny the claim.

00:17:22.622 --> 00:17:25.361
And then you’ll blame
the insurance company.

00:17:26.052 --> 00:17:28.385
But the truth is, the mistake
was yours because you lied.

00:17:29.187 --> 00:17:30.210
So never fall into that trap.

00:17:30.234 --> 00:17:36.346
All these offline agents you see
who have opened insurance shops—

00:17:36.583 --> 00:17:37.472
do you know what they do?

00:17:37.496 --> 00:17:39.861
They tell you, ‘If there’s
any issue, don’t mention it.’

00:17:39.885 --> 00:17:42.416
Their only goal is to sell the
policy somehow, by any means.

00:17:42.440 --> 00:17:43.987
So keep this in mind.

00:17:44.011 --> 00:17:46.732
Never give any wrong information.

00:17:47.216 --> 00:17:48.479
Always disclose everything honestly—

00:17:48.503 --> 00:17:50.035
only then does it make sense
to buy an insurance policy.

00:17:50.249 --> 00:17:54.241
Now, the next benefit is
pre-hospitalization coverage for 60 days

00:17:54.447 --> 00:17:56.550
and post-hospitalization
coverage for 90 days.

00:17:57.177 --> 00:17:59.510
For example, suppose
someone has a lung problem.

00:18:00.240 --> 00:18:04.248
It’s not like they go to
the doctor once in OPD

00:18:04.273 --> 00:18:08.352
and the doctor immediately says,
‘Let’s admit you and do surgery.’

00:18:08.376 --> 00:18:09.177
That doesn’t happen.

00:18:09.558 --> 00:18:11.867
First, the doctor will prescribe medicines.

00:18:11.891 --> 00:18:14.239
The patient will visit the doctor
for a few days and take medicines.

00:18:14.263 --> 00:18:17.548
If the medicines don’t help, then the doctor
will admit the patient and treat them.

00:18:17.674 --> 00:18:20.190
Even after discharge, the patient
will still need medicines for some time.

00:18:20.325 --> 00:18:26.207
So, the hospitalization expense will obviously
be covered and you will get that claim.

00:18:26.659 --> 00:18:29.865
But you should also keep the bills
for medicines taken up to 60 days

00:18:29.889 --> 00:18:31.222
before admission.

00:18:32.015 --> 00:18:33.515
And even after discharge,

00:18:33.539 --> 00:18:36.817
keep the bills for medicines taken
for 90 days after hospitalization.

00:18:36.841 --> 00:18:39.230
This is because the insurance company
will reimburse pre-hospitalization expenses

00:18:39.254 --> 00:18:46.184
for up to 60 days and post-hospitalization
expenses for up to 90 days.

00:18:46.208 --> 00:18:49.010
So the company clearly mentions:
pre-hospitalization expenses – 60 days,

00:18:49.034 --> 00:18:51.169
post-hospitalization expenses – 90 days.

00:18:51.582 --> 00:18:55.622
Some companies offer even better
coverage, like 180 days pre-hospitalization

00:18:55.709 --> 00:18:59.566
and 180 days post-hospitalization,
which is obviously better for us.

00:18:59.590 --> 00:19:02.407
So when you compare policies,
compare them according to your needs.

00:19:02.431 --> 00:19:05.303
Right now, we are just understanding
the features for educational purposes.

00:19:06.334 --> 00:19:08.509
Next comes day-care treatment.

00:19:08.756 --> 00:19:10.192
What is day-care treatment?

00:19:10.216 --> 00:19:14.167
We already discussed OPD, where
the benefit was limited to ₹3,500.

00:19:14.452 --> 00:19:17.777
But for day-care treatment, the policy says
that all day-care treatments are covered

00:19:17.801 --> 00:19:19.086
up to the sum insured.

00:19:19.252 --> 00:19:23.451
If the sum insured is ₹25 lakh, then day-care
treatments are covered up to ₹25 lakh.

00:19:23.919 --> 00:19:27.689
OPD means visiting a doctor
and getting a consultation slip,

00:19:27.713 --> 00:19:32.745
which could cost ₹100, ₹500, ₹1,000, or even
₹5,000—it depends on the city you live in.

00:19:33.245 --> 00:19:35.316
Day-care treatments are those treatments

00:19:35.340 --> 00:19:38.125
where you do not need to be admitted
to the hospital for more than 24 hours.

00:19:38.219 --> 00:19:41.910
For example, if someone
has a very high fever

00:19:41.934 --> 00:19:43.457
and needs to be admitted for a few hours
to receive two or three glucose drips,

00:19:43.481 --> 00:19:45.481
all of that will be
covered under this policy.

00:19:45.624 --> 00:19:50.174
Or if someone needs
cataract surgery for the eyes—

00:19:50.198 --> 00:19:56.191
like my mother did—this
usually happens with age.

00:19:56.293 --> 00:19:58.730
You go in, the lens is implanted
within about half an hour,

00:19:58.754 --> 00:20:00.206
and you can come back home
the same day without any problem.

00:20:00.230 --> 00:20:04.912
Similarly, if someone has kidney-related
issues and needs dialysis, or chemotherapy,

00:20:04.936 --> 00:20:10.253
or an endoscopy—any treatment that does not
require hospitalization beyond 24 hours—

00:20:10.324 --> 00:20:13.038
then all such day-care
treatments are covered,

00:20:13.062 --> 00:20:15.672
and you can claim expenses
up to ₹25 lakh under this policy.

00:20:16.204 --> 00:20:19.783
Hospitalization at home: suppose
a doctor comes to your home,

00:20:19.807 --> 00:20:21.807
then a claim may be
allowed—but not under this policy.

00:20:21.831 --> 00:20:23.338
If you are considering another policy,

00:20:23.362 --> 00:20:24.401
you should check whether
this feature is included.

00:20:24.425 --> 00:20:29.176
Ambulance charges are
covered up to actual expenses.

00:20:29.201 --> 00:20:33.517
That means if the ambulance charges
you ₹7,000, you can claim the full ₹7,000.

00:20:33.691 --> 00:20:39.421
Cashless hospitals: this policy has
tie-ups with 13,721 hospitals across India.

00:20:39.532 --> 00:20:42.596
If you go to any of these hospitals, you
don’t have to pay anything from your pocket.

00:20:42.620 --> 00:20:46.762
You can directly get admitted, and the
amount will be deducted from your policy.

00:20:46.968 --> 00:20:51.968
In hospitals that do not have a tie-up,
you first have to pay from your own pocket

00:20:51.992 --> 00:20:53.833
and then claim reimbursement later.

00:20:53.857 --> 00:20:58.928
Nowadays, most good hospitals
have tie-ups with insurance companies.

00:20:58.953 --> 00:21:00.190
Still, you should always check

00:21:00.214 --> 00:21:02.404
whether the health insurance
policy you are buying

00:21:02.642 --> 00:21:08.739
has tie-ups with the good
hospitals near your home.

00:21:08.763 --> 00:21:11.207
If there are no tie-ups near your area,

00:21:11.231 --> 00:21:13.397
then even if the company claims to have
tie-ups with 20,000 hospitals across India,

00:21:13.524 --> 00:21:15.397
the policy is not very useful for you.

00:21:15.571 --> 00:21:17.040
Next: value-added services.

00:21:17.064 --> 00:21:20.548
This is a women-specific policy, and
it mentions mid-year member addition.

00:21:21.358 --> 00:21:24.461
No matter how long this video gets, I
will explain each feature one by one.

00:21:25.103 --> 00:21:30.564
The child’s age should be 91 days at the time
of addition to the policy after issuance.

00:21:30.588 --> 00:21:34.881
For example, a woman
has taken this policy.

00:21:35.118 --> 00:21:36.872
Later, she has a baby.

00:21:37.634 --> 00:21:40.880
After 91 days from the baby’s birth,

00:21:41.149 --> 00:21:45.578
she can add the baby to the policy
and convert it into a family floater.

00:21:46.325 --> 00:21:50.966
Earlier, the policy was only for the
woman—this is called an individual policy.

00:21:51.806 --> 00:21:56.164
A family floater policy includes,
for example, you, your husband,

00:21:56.188 --> 00:22:01.077
and your child under a single policy.

00:22:01.379 --> 00:22:06.216
If an individual policy is ₹25 lakh,
that cover is only for one person.

00:22:06.240 --> 00:22:09.517
But if you take a family
floater policy of ₹50 lakh,

00:22:10.128 --> 00:22:16.306
then anyone in the family
can use that ₹50 lakh,

00:22:16.330 --> 00:22:18.870
and the entire family gets
coverage under one sum insured.

00:22:19.036 --> 00:22:22.235
If all three members took separate ₹50
lakh policies, it would be very expensive.

00:22:22.401 --> 00:22:23.306
With a family floater,

00:22:23.330 --> 00:22:26.131
the assumption is that the entire
family will not fall sick at the same time.

00:22:26.155 --> 00:22:28.036
That’s why a family floater provides
good coverage at a lower cost

00:22:28.171 --> 00:22:29.909
and is generally better for families.

00:22:29.933 --> 00:22:31.361
But keep one thing in mind:

00:22:32.027 --> 00:22:35.908
if you are taking a family floater
policy, do not add your parents to it.

00:22:35.932 --> 00:22:39.797
For example, suppose the husband
is 31 years old, the wife is 30 years old,

00:22:39.908 --> 00:22:41.972
and the baby is 1 year old.

00:22:42.805 --> 00:22:46.718
In this case, the highest age
is the husband’s, which is 31.

00:22:46.964 --> 00:22:48.733
So the premium for the
entire family floater policy

00:22:48.757 --> 00:22:51.502
will be calculated based on the age of 31.

00:22:51.803 --> 00:22:54.017
The higher the age, the higher the premium.

00:22:54.144 --> 00:22:58.509
Now, suppose you also add
your father, who is 65 years old,

00:22:58.874 --> 00:23:02.199
and your grandmother, who is 62 years old.

00:23:02.389 --> 00:23:06.255
Then the premium for all five members
will be calculated based on the age of 65.

00:23:06.279 --> 00:23:09.358
Where the premium should have been
around ₹15,000, it may become ₹45,000.

00:23:10.246 --> 00:23:12.826
That is why you should take a
separate family floater policy for parents,

00:23:12.850 --> 00:23:15.302
and separate policies for
people in the same age group.

00:23:15.326 --> 00:23:16.881
A child’s age does not
make much difference.

00:23:18.286 --> 00:23:22.193
Next, it says that you can add your
child to this policy only after 91 days.

00:23:22.217 --> 00:23:25.209
Second, the spouse will
be added after 91 days.

00:23:25.242 --> 00:23:29.847
Similarly, if a woman has taken this
policy and wants to add her spouse,

00:23:29.871 --> 00:23:31.871
the spouse can also be
added only after 91 days.

00:23:32.712 --> 00:23:33.968
Then comes the free
health check-up benefit.

00:23:33.992 --> 00:23:37.116
Female members get free health
check-ups worth ₹3,000 per year.

00:23:39.104 --> 00:23:42.052
A male cannot buy this policy alone
because it is a women-specific policy.

00:23:42.076 --> 00:23:45.147
However, if it is converted into a family
floater, a male member can be added.

00:23:45.281 --> 00:23:47.512
In that case, the male member
will get a benefit of ₹1,000 per year

00:23:47.536 --> 00:23:49.703
for free health check-ups.

00:23:50.650 --> 00:23:51.475
E-consultation:

00:23:51.499 --> 00:23:56.992
You can consult a gynecologist as many
times as you want through this application.

00:23:57.121 --> 00:23:59.739
These consultations are done
via the Star Tele Health app.

00:23:59.818 --> 00:24:03.047
Nowadays, such e-consultations
are available on phones,

00:24:03.071 --> 00:24:04.880
though in my opinion they
are not always very useful.

00:24:05.046 --> 00:24:09.506
Daily cash allowance: you will receive
₹2,000 per day as a cash allowance,

00:24:09.601 --> 00:24:11.054
for a maximum of 7 days.

00:24:11.078 --> 00:24:14.284
That means ₹2,000 will be credited
to your account daily, up to seven days.

00:24:14.308 --> 00:24:15.117
When will this apply?

00:24:15.141 --> 00:24:19.466
In cases where the insured person
is occupying shared accommodation.

00:24:19.490 --> 00:24:23.577
For example, if you are admitted to a
hospital and do not get a private room

00:24:23.791 --> 00:24:27.299
and instead stay in a twin-sharing room,

00:24:27.552 --> 00:24:35.459
the insurance company will pay
you ₹2,000 per day, as per this policy.

00:24:35.728 --> 00:24:37.324
Now let’s understand the maternity benefit.

00:24:38.451 --> 00:24:41.269
Since this policy has a
sum insured of ₹25 lakh,

00:24:41.348 --> 00:24:44.618
the maternity benefit is up to ₹75,000.

00:24:44.642 --> 00:24:46.412
This is for female.

00:24:47.071 --> 00:24:50.373
The waiting period for
this benefit is one year.

00:24:50.397 --> 00:24:54.714
Although the waiting period for pre-existing
diseases under this policy is two years,

00:24:54.738 --> 00:24:57.079
the waiting period for the
maternity benefit is only one year.

00:24:57.325 --> 00:24:59.325
So who is this policy good for?

00:24:59.349 --> 00:25:02.134
It is a good option for newly married women

00:25:02.158 --> 00:25:06.721
or for those who are planning
to conceive after one year.

00:25:06.745 --> 00:25:09.808
If you take this policy today,
complete the one-year waiting period,

00:25:09.832 --> 00:25:11.458
and then have a delivery,

00:25:11.482 --> 00:25:21.237
your health insurance policy will
cover maternity expenses up to ₹75,000—

00:25:21.261 --> 00:25:26.332
whether it is a normal
delivery or a C-section.

00:25:26.482 --> 00:25:29.204
Women’s plans are considered good mainly

00:25:29.346 --> 00:25:30.759
because maternity is something
almost everyone needs.

00:25:30.783 --> 00:25:33.402
After a certain age, most
women plan to become mothers.

00:25:33.719 --> 00:25:38.608
So if you are in that age group—25, 26,
27, or so—and depending on your situation,

00:25:38.719 --> 00:25:40.854
whether you are already
married or planning to get married,

00:25:40.878 --> 00:25:42.878
you should definitely look
at the maternity benefit.

00:25:43.941 --> 00:25:46.767
Here, after a one-year waiting period,
you get a maternity benefit of ₹75,000.

00:25:46.917 --> 00:25:50.973
Even if you take a ₹50 lakh policy, the
maternity benefit will still be ₹75,000.

00:25:51.076 --> 00:25:54.187
The same applies if you take a ₹75
lakh policy—the benefit remains ₹75,000.

00:25:54.211 --> 00:25:55.369
There is another case
shown in a screenshot.

00:25:55.393 --> 00:25:59.472
If you take a ₹1 crore policy,
your sum insured remains ₹1 crore,

00:25:59.496 --> 00:26:05.414
and the maternity benefit increases to ₹1
lakh, again after a one-year waiting period.

00:26:05.438 --> 00:26:08.739
The benefit is available for
a maximum of two deliveries.

00:26:08.764 --> 00:26:11.384
That means you can get up
to ₹1 lakh for the first delivery

00:26:11.408 --> 00:26:13.233
and up to ₹1 lakh for the second delivery.

00:26:13.257 --> 00:26:15.257
From the third delivery onward,
this benefit will not be available.

00:26:15.408 --> 00:26:19.353
If you take a ₹15 lakh policy,

00:26:19.471 --> 00:26:24.590
the maternity benefit will be
₹50,000, with a one-year waiting period.

00:26:24.955 --> 00:26:30.156
If you take a ₹10 lakh policy,
the benefit will still be ₹50,000,

00:26:30.180 --> 00:26:32.180
but the waiting period becomes two years.

00:26:32.378 --> 00:26:35.584
If you take an even lower
policy, such as ₹5 lakh,

00:26:35.694 --> 00:26:37.980
the maternity benefit will be only ₹25,000,

00:26:38.004 --> 00:26:39.797
and the waiting period
will again be two years.

00:26:40.352 --> 00:26:45.315
One more important point:
if you take a ₹25 lakh policy,

00:26:46.069 --> 00:26:50.579
along with the ₹75,000 maternity
benefit and a one-year waiting period,

00:26:50.610 --> 00:26:53.150
the policy also offers newborn baby cover.

00:26:53.404 --> 00:26:57.562
The newborn baby is
covered up to ₹6,25,000,

00:26:57.586 --> 00:27:03.142
which is one-fourth of
the ₹25 lakh sum insured.

00:27:03.784 --> 00:27:10.565
Nowadays, if a baby is born
and, in case of any complications,

00:27:10.589 --> 00:27:14.144
the baby needs treatment, the
baby may not have a separate policy.

00:27:14.168 --> 00:27:15.477
In such cases, this policy provides

00:27:15.501 --> 00:27:21.930
coverage of up to ₹6,25,000 for
the newborn immediately after birth.

00:27:21.954 --> 00:27:24.144
It is written here that a
newborn baby is covered.

00:27:24.168 --> 00:27:27.771
I hope, friends, that I’ve
followed the right approach.

00:27:27.795 --> 00:27:30.374
This way, you’re gaining knowledge as
we go through everything step by step,

00:27:30.398 --> 00:27:32.279
and it helps you understand
health insurance clearly.

00:27:32.572 --> 00:27:34.262
If you like this approach,

00:27:34.286 --> 00:27:38.238
please support the channel by
